  <h2>Why Most Regression Suites Stop Working Over Time</h2>
  <p>A regression suite starts out useful. Six months later, it's a source of noise. The CI job takes 45 minutes. Tests fail intermittently for no apparent reason. Developers stop looking at CI results because false positives have trained them to ignore failures. The suite that was supposed to catch regressions has become the thing everyone works around.</p>
  <p>This pattern is almost never about the initial test quality. It is about maintenance debt accumulating faster than it is paid down. The root causes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Test fragility:</strong> Tests written against unstable selectors or tight UI dependencies break when the UI changes, even when the underlying behavior hasn't changed.</li>
    <li><strong>No ownership:</strong> "Shared ownership" of a test suite means nobody owns it. Tests written by people who have since left the company, for features that have since been redesigned, never get removed.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth without pruning:</strong> New tests get added every sprint. Old tests that no longer reflect the current application stay in the suite. The suite grows but its signal-to-noise ratio falls.</li>
    <li><strong>No execution time target:</strong> Without a target, suites grow to fill whatever time is available. A suite that takes 90 minutes gets run less often and eventually gets bypassed before releases.</li>
  </ul>

